## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to secure public Cloud Run endpoints for multiple partners using standard GCP controls.

## Key Concepts
- Least-privilege IAM per partner
- IAP for authenticating users to Cloud Run
- Per-partner isolation via separate service accounts or gateway policies
- Credential rotation and auditability
- Synthetic tests to validate misconfig denial

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo test: simulate non-permitted access and expect HTTP 403
const token = obtainTokenFor('partnerX');
fetch('https://api.example.run.app/endpoint', { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` }})
  .then(response => {
    if (response.status === 403) {
      console.log('✓ Access properly blocked for unauthorized partner');
    } else {
      console.error('✗ Security misconfiguration detected');
    }
  });
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of multi-tenant secrets isolation in GCP using IAM Conditions, along with knowledge of auditability, safe rollout practices, and CI-based validation. It tests practical skills in designing secure tenant boundaries at runtime.

## Key Concepts
- **IAM Conditions**: Use IAM Conditions on service account keys to grant Secret Manager access only when the request matches specific tenant identifiers
- **Tenant-scoped naming**: Prefix secret names with tenant IDs and apply labels for enforcement (e.g., projects/PROJECT/secrets/{tenant_id}-api-key)
- **Audit logging**: Configure Cloud Audit Logs with filtering and alerting for cross-tenant access patterns
- **CI validation**: Automated testing that provisions synthetic tenants, attempts cross-tenant access, and rolls back on failure

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: derive tenant-scoped secret path and attach conditional binding
const tenantId = "t1";
const secretName = `projects/PROJECT/secrets/${tenantId}-api-key/versions/latest`;

// IAM Condition for tenant-specific access
const condition = {
  expression: "resource.name == 'projects/PROJECT/secrets/' + tenantId + '-api-key'",
  title: "Tenant " + tenantId + " Secret Access"
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this approach to hundreds or thousands of tenants while maintaining manageability?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical tenancy controls in a NoSQL store; checks how to enforce data isolation with security rules, IAM, and network boundaries; assesses ability to design automated containment.

## Key Concepts
- Firestore security rules and tenant-scoped paths
- Per-tenant service accounts with least privilege
- Private Service Connect and VPC Service Controls
- CI/CD gate with synthetic tenants and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Firestore rules snippet (illustrative)
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /tenants/{tenantId}/{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.token.tenants.contains(tenantId);
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for potential rule bypasses in CI?
- What are the trade-offs of using per-tenant collections vs single collection with tenant_id field?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical containment design across IAM, network perimeters, key management, and rollback validation using synthetic tenants.

## Key Concepts
- IAM conditions for tenant isolation
- Perimeter controls: PSC/VC and VPC Service Controls
- CMEK rotation and data-key isolation
- Automated rollback with synthetic tenants and validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: conditional binding to isolate tenant access
const policy = { bindings: [] };
policy.bindings.push({
  role: "roles/storage.objectViewer",
  members: ["user:tenant-a@example.com"],
  condition: {
    title: "Tenant Access Restriction",
    description: "Restrict access to tenant-specific resources",
    expression: "resource.name.startsWith('projects/_/buckets/tenant-a-')"
  }
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design secure, multi-tenant API access patterns using ephemeral tokens, fine-grained IAM controls, and comprehensive security automation. It tests understanding of GCP security primitives, tenant isolation strategies, and operational readiness.

## Key Concepts
- Centralized token issuance with short-lived JWTs
- IAM Conditions for tenant-scoped resource access
- BigQuery Authorized Views for data-level isolation
- API Gateway JWT validation and routing
- Per-tenant CMEK encryption for data protection
- Automated validation and rollback procedures

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example IAM policy snippet (illustrative)
{
  "bindings": [
    {
      "role": "roles/bigquery.dataViewer",
      "members": [
        "serviceAccount:tenant-access@project.iam.gserviceaccount.com"
      ],
      "condition": {
        "title": "Tenant Dataset Access",
        "expression": "resource.name.startsWith('projects/project/datasets/tenant-')"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```
